# `print 5` is refused at compile time, naming the argument, its type and where
# it is. `text/chars 5` was not, and answered a bare runtime string naming
# neither the call site nor the value — a worse error for no reason a reader
# could predict.
#
# The cause was the guard that keeps a program's own `run` from inheriting the
# builtin's demands. A std wrapper is a declaration too, so every one of them
# shadowed the builtin it forwards to, and which builtins got a good message
# came down to whether somebody had written a wrapper over them.
#
# `chopped 5` is refused too: a rename of a rename is still a rename, so the
# reader's own one-line wrapper over `text/chars` carries the builtin's
# demand to its call site. The boundary that remains is dataflow — a literal
# bound to a name first, or an arm that does anything besides forward, is
# past what the check reads.
import "std/text"

fn chopped s
  text/chars s

pub play = print "{chopped 5} {text/split 5 ","} {text/bytes 5}"
